## Key Financial Factors on HackerOne## 1. Impact Dictates the Dollar [9] 
A buffer overflow in an internal, non-production test environment might earn a Low reward ($50 to $1,000) or simply be closed as "Informational". Conversely, the exact same memory vulnerability inside a core product API that allows full Remote Code Execution (RCE) elevates the report to Critical, commanding $20,000 to $30,000+. [7, 10] 
## 2. General vs. Internet Bug Bounty (IBB) Rates

* Private/Corporate Programs: Top-tier enterprise programs pay premium rates. For example, a severe data-handling flow can pay up to $30,000 on [PayPal's Bug Bounty Program](https://hackerone.com/paypal). [7] 
* The Internet Bug Bounty (IBB): HackerOne's open-source collaborative pool. Following a significant rewards restructuring, standard open-source variable vulnerabilities are capped much lower:
* Critical: ~$2,257
   * High: ~$1,009
   * Medium: ~$297 [11, 12, 13] 

## 3. AI and Prompt-Variable Injection [14] 
Corporate scopes are shifting toward large language model (LLM) infrastructure. Manipulating user-facing variables to corrupt system prompt variables (Prompt Injection) regularly yields $1,000 to $30,000, depending on whether the system leaks back-end database records or performs unauthenticated commands. [15, 16] 
